  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above normal temperatures continue with the first \nrain chances in two weeks Thursday and Friday.\n\nBroad high pressure over the western Atlantic will continue to \nmaintain above normal temperatures and quiet conditions through this \nevening. Expect light winds out of the south and southeast (gusting \n15-20 mph) yielding highs for most in the low to mid 70s. A few \nlocations could punch into the upper 70s in the lee of Alleghenies \nand south/east of Washington, D.C. this afternoon given increased \nsoutherly flow.  After some patchy fog to start, expect mostly sunny \nskies to prevail giving way to increasing cloud cover during the \nafternoon and evening as the incumbent trough/front approach from \nthe Ohio River Valley. \n\nAfter a 2+ week hiatus, rain chances return from the west late \ntonight into Thursday and Friday. This is largely due in part to the \nridge of high pressure shunting east and an incumbent trough/front \nentering from the upper Great Lakes and Ohio River Valley. Model \nconsensus continues to trend downward on widespread rain chances \nwith this system given the bulk of the forcing north and west of the \nregion. Most of the guidance has low pressure ejecting out of the \nnorthern Great Lakes into eastern Ontario with it's associated cold \nfront ejecting from the Ohio River Valley Thursday morning into \nThursday afternoon. The front will stall along and east of I-95 \nThursday night into Friday. Another area of low pressure will \ndevelop along the front down across the Carolinas riding north to \nthe Delmarva and southern New England coast Friday afternoon into \nSaturday morning. As a result, expect the greatest rain chances \nalong west of the Alleghenies and in areas along/east of I-95 with \nsomewhat of a skip in between. Rainfall amounts will range between a \nquarter to a half an inch areawide through Friday with locally \nheavier totals along and east of I-95 (southern MD) as well as over \nthe mountains. Highs will remain in the upper 60s and low 70s \nThursday and Friday despite the added cloud cover (low 60s \nmountains). West to northwest flow may contribute to some \ndownsloping east of the mountains leading to the warmer conditions. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler temperatures and mountain showers expected \nthis weekend as another strong front crosses early next week. \n\nOne upper level trough exits with a more potent one to follow toward \nthe back half of the weekend into early next week. Upslope showers \nmay linger over the mountains and in locations east of I-95 Saturday \nmorning as the upper level trough/cold front push offshore. Weak \nhigh pressure will build from the south Saturday afternoon into \nSunday ahead of a more pedant trough heading into the first half of \nnext week. Once again the main forcing with this system will be up \ntoward the Hudson Bay of Canada while the resultant surface fronts \ncross the region. Overall guidance has trended downward in \nprecipitation with this system with any shower chances Sunday and \nMonday confined to the mountains. These areas have a better bet of \nprecipitation given the terrain influence compared to the locations \nfurther east where any mentionable forcing is lacking. Broad 1020-\n1022 mb high pressure builds back in from the southeastern US Monday \ninto Tuesday with another cold front to follow by the middle of next \nweek. Overall synoptic pattern turns zonal for much of next week. \n\nSeasonable temperatures are expected to return this weekend and into \nmuch of next week. Highs will settle back into the upper 50s and mid \n60s (40s mountains). Overnight lows will fall back into the 30s and \n40s.",
